# `integ::QuadraResult`

The value of a one-variable integral with its error estimate and the work spent.

`value` is a `Series` (a scalar for a plain integrand, one entry per batch row for a batched one). `error_estimate` is the change between the last two resolution levels, `evaluations` the number of nodes the integrand was called on in total (including levels discarded by `Auto`), `refinements` the number of doublings, `converged` whether the change fell within the tolerance, and `rule_used` the rule that produced `value`, which differs from `options.rule` only for `QuadraRule::Auto`.

## Fields

### `value`

`pluss::Series`, default `undefined`.

The integral.

### `error_estimate`

`double`, default `+inf`.

Change between the last two resolution levels.

### `evaluations`

`std::int64_t`, default `0`.

Total nodes the integrand was called on.

### `refinements`

`std::int64_t`, default `0`.

Resolution doublings.

### `converged`

`bool`, default `false`.

Whether the change fell within `tolerance`.

### `rule_used`

`integ::QuadraRule`, default `Trapezoidal`.

The rule that produced `value`.

## Guidance

Returned by the one-variable `quadra`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Reading a result.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(12));
  const integ::QuadraResult r = integ::quadra([](const Series &t) { return t.exp(); }, 0.0, 1.0);
  out.writeln("value =", r.value.item<double>(), ", error estimate =", r.error_estimate);
  out.writeln("evaluations =", r.evaluations, ", refinements =", r.refinements, ", converged =", r.converged, ", rule used =", static_cast<int>(r.rule_used));
}
```

### Example 2

Not converged. A tight tolerance with no refinement budget leaves `converged` false, and with no second level to compare against the error estimate stays at infinity.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  integ::QuadraOptions options;
  options.tolerance = 1e-14;
  options.max_refinements = 0;
  options.nodes = 5;
  const integ::QuadraResult r = integ::quadra([](const Series &t) { return t.exp(); }, 0.0, 1.0, options);
  out.writeln("converged =", r.converged, ", error estimate =", r.error_estimate, ", value =", r.value.item<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

A batch. The value has one entry per row of the integrand.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  const Series k = pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0}).reshape({2, 1});
  const integ::QuadraResult r = integ::quadra([&](const Series &t) { return t.reshape({1, t.numel()}).mul(k).exp(); }, 0.0, 1.0);
  out.writeln("shape =", r.value.sizes(), ", values =", r.value.items<double>());
}
```

